@using PlaywrightDungeonCrawl.Dungeon
@inject IConfiguration Config
@{
    var starterUrl = StarterKit.RepoUrl(Config);
    var dungeonUrl = $"{Context.Request.Scheme}://{Context.Request.Host}";
}
<section>
    <p>
        No experience needed. By the end of this floor you'll have Visual Studio installed, a test
        project open, and a test of your own driving a browser through the dungeon. Every floor after
        this one adds a test to the same project.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>1. Install Visual Studio</h2>
    <ol>
        <li>Download <strong>Visual Studio Community</strong> (free) from
            <a href="https://visualstudio.microsoft.com/vs/community/">visualstudio.microsoft.com</a> and run the installer.</li>
        <li>When it asks what to install, tick the <strong>.NET desktop development</strong> workload.
            That brings the .NET SDK, the test tools and Git with it.</li>
        <li>Click <strong>Install</strong>. It takes a while, so it's a good time for a snack.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="tip">
        <strong>On a Mac or Linux?</strong> Use <a href="https://code.visualstudio.com/">VS Code</a> with the
        <em>C# Dev Kit</em> extension and the <a href="https://dotnet.microsoft.com/download">.NET 10 SDK</a>.
        The steps below are the same idea; the menus differ.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>2. Clone the starter kit</h2>
    <p>The starter kit is a test project that's already set up for this dungeon.</p>
    <ol>
        <li>Open Visual Studio. On the start window, choose <strong>Clone a repository</strong>.</li>
        <li>For <em>Repository location</em>, paste:<br /><code>@starterUrl</code></li>
        <li>Pick a folder to put it in and click <strong>Clone</strong>.</li>
        <li>Visual Studio opens the solution for you. In <strong>Solution Explorer</strong> you should see
            <em>Solution 'DungeonCrawler' (1 of 1 project)</em> with a project called
            <strong>DungeonCrawler.Tests</strong> underneath.</li>
        <li>Seeing a list of folders instead? Double-click <strong>DungeonCrawler.slnx</strong> to open the solution.</li>
    </ol>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>3. Point it at this dungeon</h2>
    <p>
        In Solution Explorer, double-click <strong>dungeon.runsettings</strong> to open it. The
        <code>DungeonUrl</code> line says where your tests will send the browser.
    </p>
    <figure class="screenshot">
        <img src="~/img/setup/visual-studio-runsettings.png" width="1600" height="742" loading="lazy"
             alt="Visual Studio with dungeon.runsettings open. The DungeonUrl line, value http://localhost:5299, is highlighted in the editor, and dungeon.runsettings is highlighted in Solution Explorer under the DungeonCrawler.Tests project." />
        <figcaption>dungeon.runsettings, open in Visual Studio. The highlighted line is the only one you'll change.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>It should match the address of this site:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;Parameter name="DungeonUrl" value="@dungeonUrl" /&gt;</code></pre>
    <p>If it's different, change it and save. That's the only setting you'll ever need to touch.</p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>4. Run the example test</h2>
    <ol>
        <li>Open <strong>Test › Test Explorer</strong>. If it's empty, build first with <strong>Build › Build Solution</strong>.</li>
        <li>You'll see <code>TheDungeonIsOpen</code> under <em>ExampleTests</em>, with a blue icon: that means "not run yet".</li>
        <li>Click <strong>Run All Tests</strong>: the double play button at the top left of Test Explorer.</li>
        <li>The very first run downloads a copy of Chromium for Playwright, which takes about a minute.</li>
        <li>A browser window opens on the dungeon map, then closes. A green tick next to
            <code>TheDungeonIsOpen</code> means your setup works.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="tip">
        <strong>Red cross saying it "can't reach the dungeon"?</strong> The address in step 3 doesn't match
        this site, or the dungeon isn't running.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>5. Slow it down with the Playwright Inspector</h2>
    <p>
        That was quick: the browser opened and closed before you could see much. Tests are meant to be
        fast, but while you're learning you'll want to watch. The <strong>Playwright Inspector</strong>
        pauses a test and lets you step through it.
    </p>
    <ol>
        <li>In Test Explorer, find <code>StepThroughWithTheInspector</code> under <em>ExampleTests</em>. It's the
            same test with one extra line at the top: <code>await Page.PauseAsync();</code></li>
        <li>Right-click it and choose <strong>Run</strong>. ("Run All" skips it on purpose, because it waits for you.)
            Two windows open: a browser showing an empty page (<code>about:blank</code>), and the
            <strong>Playwright Inspector</strong> showing your test's code. The test is paused on
            <code>PauseAsync</code>, before the dungeon has even loaded.</li>
    </ol>
    <figure class="screenshot narrow">
        <img src="~/img/setup/playwright-inspector.png" width="1200" height="1132" loading="lazy"
             alt="The Playwright Inspector, paused on line 34 of ExampleTests.cs. Numbered markers: 1 on the Step over button, 2 on the Pick locator button, 3 on the Resume button, 4 on the Log tab, which lists Pause done in 1ms, Navigate to localhost:5299 done in 130ms, and ToHaveTitleAsync paused." />
        <figcaption>The Inspector mid-test. (1) Step over, (2) Pick locator, (3) Resume, (4) the Log of what's happened so far.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>Now walk through it, one click of <strong>Step over</strong> (1) at a time:</p>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>First click</strong> finishes the pause. The page is still blank: nothing has asked it to load yet.</li>
        <li><strong>Second click</strong> runs <code>EnterAsync("/")</code>. The dungeon appears in the browser.</li>
        <li>Stop here and try <strong>Pick locator</strong> (2). Hover over the big heading on the page and click it.
            The <em>Locator</em> tab shows the C# to find it, something like
            <code>GetByRole(AriaRole.Heading, new() { Name = "The Respawn Room" })</code>. You'll lean on this a lot
            on floor 2.</li>
        <li><strong>Two more clicks</strong> run the two checks. Open the <strong>Log</strong> tab (4) to see every
            step so far, each with a tick and how long it took.</li>
        <li><strong>One last click</strong> finishes the test. The browser closes, and Test Explorer shows a green tick.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
        In a hurry? <strong>Resume</strong> (3) runs everything that's left in one go.
    </p>
    <p class="tip">
        <strong>Want to pause one of your own tests?</strong> Add <code>await Page.PauseAsync();</code> wherever you
        want it to stop. Delete the line when you're done: a paused test waits for you forever, and Test Explorer
        will look stuck.
    </p>
    <p>
        Prefer watching a whole run at normal pace? In <code>dungeon.runsettings</code>, turn on
        <code>SlowMo</code> (the comments in the file explain how). It waits a second after every action.
        Turn it off again before the timed trap rooms.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>6. Write your first test</h2>
    <p>
        This floor's trap room is a door that refuses to open for humans. It only opens for a browser that a
        test is driving. Before you face it, warm up on this training dummy. It's just as picky: poke it
        yourself and see.
    </p>
    <div class="training-dummy">
        <p class="dummy-art" aria-hidden="true">🎯</p>
        <button type="button">Poke</button>
        <p class="dummy-voice" data-testid="dummy-voice" aria-live="polite"></p>
    </div>
    <script src="~/js/training-dummy.js" asp-append-version="true"></script>
    <p>Now poke it with a test:</p>
    <ol>
        <li>In Solution Explorer, right-click <strong>DungeonCrawler.Tests</strong> and choose <strong>Add › Class</strong>.</li>
        <li>Name it <code>TrainingDummyTests.cs</code> and click <strong>Add</strong>.</li>
        <li>Replace everything in the new file with:</li>
    </ol>
<pre><code>namespace DungeonCrawler.Tests;

public class TrainingDummyTests : DungeonTest
{
    [Test]
    public async Task PokeTheDummy()
    {
        // Open this scroll, where the dummy stands.
        await EnterAsync("/floors/setup");

        // Find the button whose label is "Poke", and click it.
        await Page.GetByRole(AriaRole.Button, new() { Name = "Poke" }).ClickAsync();

        // Check the dummy flinched. Expect(...) waits for it to answer.
        await Expect(Page.GetByTestId("dummy-voice")).ToContainTextAsync("Ow!");
    }
}</code></pre>
    <p>What's going on:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><code>: DungeonTest</code> gives your class a browser tab called <code>Page</code> and the dungeon helpers.</li>
        <li><code>[Test]</code> tells Test Explorer this method is a test.</li>
        <li><code>await</code> means "wait for this step to finish before the next one". Every Playwright action needs it.</li>
        <li><code>GetByRole</code> finds an element the way a person would: by what it is (a button) and what it says.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Build (<strong>Ctrl+Shift+B</strong>), then in Test Explorer right-click <code>PokeTheDummy</code> and choose
        <strong>Run</strong>. Watch the browser poke the dummy. A green tick means it flinched.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>7. Knock on the Welcome Matt</h2>
    <p>
        Now the real thing. The <a href="/rooms/welcome-matt">Welcome Matt</a> is this floor's trap room: the same
        kind of trap as the dummy, in a different place. Write a second test, in a new class called
        <code>WelcomeMattTests</code>, that does what the dummy test does, with three changes:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Where:</strong> the door is at <code>/rooms/welcome-matt</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>What to click:</strong> visit the door and read its button, or use <strong>Pick locator</strong> from step 5.</li>
        <li><strong>How it ends:</strong> instead of checking for a reply, finish with <code>await TakeTreasureAsync();</code>.
            It waits for the treasure flag to appear and prints it in the test's output.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="tip">
        <strong>Stuck?</strong> The room's <em>Give me a nudge</em> button has hints, and on this floor they're free.
    </p>
    <p class="tip">
        <strong>Did it work?</strong> When your test takes the treasure, the browser stays open for 3 seconds so
        you can see the door give it up. Change or turn off that pause with <code>VictoryPauseSeconds</code> in
        <code>dungeon.runsettings</code>. To watch every step instead, add <code>await Page.PauseAsync();</code>
        at the top of the test and step through it in the Inspector (step 5), or turn on <code>SlowMo</code>.
        The Welcome Matt gives you 10 minutes, so there's no rush.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>8. Claim the treasure</h2>
    <ol>
        <li>Run your new test. When it goes green, click it in Test Explorer.</li>
        <li>In the details pane, open the test's additional output. You'll see <code>Treasure flag: PDC{…}</code>.</li>
        <li>Copy the flag, including the braces, and paste it into <a href="/#treasure">Claim treasure</a> on the Trophy Wall in the Respawn Room.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Then answer this floor's riddle, and take the stairs down.</p>
</section>
